1
00:00:00,901 --> 00:00:22,285
SlokAbhyasa is a small app for learning slokas by ear. You record a verse, or load one from a file. The app listens, remembers it, and later tells you how closely your own recitation matches. Everything runs on your computer; nothing is uploaded.

2
00:00:22,595 --> 00:00:41,891
This library holds the learner's own recordings: the twenty slokas of chapter twelve of the Bhagavad Gita, Bhakti Yoga, together with its opening and closing lines, and one sloka from chapter eighteen. They are ordinary WAV files, kept in one folder per chapter.

3
00:00:43,031 --> 00:00:55,055
Each recording can keep its words beside it, as a text file. The text is written on this computer by the Whisper speech model, and you can correct it by hand.

4
00:00:57,994 --> 00:01:09,610
The Player plays any recording at any speed, with the pitch preserved. Slow a sloka down to three quarters, or half, and catch every syllable.

5
00:01:14,442 --> 00:01:31,242
To teach the app a new sloka, record it, or pick a file. Here is a recording of chapter eighteen, sloka sixty six, the famous closing sloka. The app trims the silence and writes down the words it hears.

6
00:01:33,924 --> 00:01:42,876
Give it a name, choose a folder, and save. The recording, its words and its analysis are stored together.

7
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:55,037
Teach mode works on one sloka at a time. Here is the third sloka of chapter twelve, with its text.

8
00:01:55,348 --> 00:01:59,596
Press Play to hear it, at half speed if you like.

9
00:02:07,995 --> 00:02:19,443
Then press Listen, and recite it yourself. For this demonstration the recitation rushes the first line, and skips a few syllables later on.

10
00:02:27,175 --> 00:02:44,551
The app lines your recitation up with the original and scores it: content, timing, pitch and dynamics. The chart shows the two side by side, and the list below names each deviation, judged against a tolerance you can set yourself.

11
00:02:46,177 --> 00:02:57,577
Your words are compared with the sloka's text too. Sloka words that were not heard are highlighted, and so are extra or different words in your attempt.

12
00:03:01,158 --> 00:03:10,782
In Self Evaluation you can tick several slokas, recite any one of them from memory, and let the app work out which one you sang.

13
00:03:11,090 --> 00:03:14,522
Here the learner recites one of the five.

14
00:03:30,854 --> 00:03:41,270
It found sloka three, opened its report, and marked the other four as not matching this recording. Every report is one click away.

15
00:03:44,376 --> 00:03:52,488
Quizzes test your memory. Choose a folder, pick one sloka or several, and name the quiz.

16
00:03:53,459 --> 00:04:01,427
The recording stays hidden; only the name is shown. You recite it from memory, in one take.

17
00:04:04,182 --> 00:04:11,238
When you stop, the app compares the take with the original, just as in Self Evaluation.

18
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:39,864
Each sloka is judged on content, pronunciation, timing, pitch and dynamics, within fixed tolerances. You choose which categories count; here, content alone. The quiz score is the share of slokas that passed.

19
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:46,566
Every attempt is saved with the quiz, so the trend shows your progress over the weeks.

20
00:04:47,827 --> 00:05:04,723
Your recordings stay on your computer as plain WAV files, easy to copy and back up. SlokAbhyasa is free and open source: github.com/hchowlur-ops/slokAbhyasa
